# The second dependency provider: dependencies that Nix has *already* placed in
# the build sandbox, described to Bazel without invoking Nix.
#
# This is what //third_party's aliases point at when --//third_party:provider is
# set to "sandbox". The other provider (//third_party/nix, via rules_nixpkgs)
# calls nix-build to realise each package; that cannot work inside a nixpkgs
# derivation, which builds with no network and no access to the Nix daemon. This
# one can, because it asks Nix for nothing.
#
# --- why these targets are empty --------------------------------------------
#
# The trick is that there is no trick: when Nix builds a derivation, the cc
# wrapper in its stdenv reads buildInputs and injects
#
#     -isystem /nix/store/<hash>-doctest-2.5.0/include
#
# into every compiler invocation, via NIX_CFLAGS_COMPILE, plus the matching -L
# and -rpath into every link via NIX_LDFLAGS. The include path is therefore
# already correct before Bazel says anything, and a target that tried to name
# the store path would be duplicating -- and pinning -- something the
# environment supplies more accurately.
#
# So a header-only dependency needs a target that contributes *nothing but its
# name*. That is not a degenerate case to apologise for; it is the point. The
# dependency edge in the Bazel graph stays real -- strict deps still enforces
# that only declared users may include the headers -- while the question of
# where the files live is answered by whoever set up the sandbox.
#
# A dependency with a library to link adds `linkopts = ["-lfoo"]` and nothing
# else: -L is already on the command line, so the bare -l resolves.
#
# --- what this costs ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Hermeticity, in Bazel's sense. These targets do not name their inputs, so
# Bazel cannot hash them, and its cache cannot tell two different doctests
# apart. That is a bad trade in a dev loop and the correct one inside a nixpkgs
# derivation, where Nix has already pinned every input by hash and the build
# runs exactly once in a sandbox it fully controls. Two different jobs; the
# --//third_party:provider flag is which one you are doing.

package(default_visibility = ["//visibility:public"])

# doctest. Header-only as used here: consumers define DOCTEST_CONFIG_IMPLEMENT
# and compile the implementation themselves, so there is nothing to link and
# not even a linkopt is needed.
cc_library(
    name = "doctest",
)
